Doubly Linked List (DLL): Insertion at Beginning
Concept:
A doubly linked list allows bidirectional traversal. Each node stores data, a pointer to the next node, and a pointer to the previous node.
To insert a node at the beginning:
-Create a new node.
-Set its next to the current head.
-Set its prev to NULL.
-Update the previous pointer of the existing head.
-Move head to point to the new node.                                                      Code:                                                                                                       #include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node *prev, *next;
};
void insertAtBeginningDLL(struct Node **head, int data) {
    struct Node *newNode = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
    newNode->data = data;
    newNode->prev = NULL;
    newNode->next = *head;
    if (*head != NULL)
        (*head)->prev = newNode;
    *head = newNode;
}                                                                                                                    Circular Linked List (CLL): Insertion at Beginning
Concept:
In a circular linked list, the next pointer of the last node links back to the head.
Insertion steps:
-Create a new node.
-If list is empty, make the node point to itself.
-Otherwise, traverse to the last node and link it to the new node.
-Set newNode->next = head and update head to newNode.
Code:                                                                                                             struct CNode {
    int data;
    struct CNode *next;
};
void insertAtBeginningCLL(struct CNode **head, int data) {
    struct CNode *newNode = (struct CNode*)malloc(sizeof(struct CNode));
    newNode->data = data;
    if (*head == NULL) {
        newNode->next = newNode;
        *head = newNode;
        return;
    }
    struct CNode *temp = *head;
    while (temp->next != *head)
        temp = temp->next;
    temp->next = newNode;
    newNode->next = *head;
    *head = newNode;
}                                                                                                                    Queue Using Linked List: Insertion at Beginning
Concept:
A queue follows the FIFO principle. Normally, elements are inserted at the rear, but this example demonstrates insertion at the beginning.
Steps:
-Create a new node.
-Set newNode->next = front.
-Update front to point to the new node.
-If the queue was empty, make rear = newNode.
Code:                                                                                                               struct QNode {
    int data;
    struct QNode *next;
};
void insertAtBeginningQueue(struct QNode **front, struct QNode **rear, int data) {
    struct QNode *newNode = (struct QNode*)malloc(sizeof(struct QNode));
    newNode->data = data;
    newNode->next = *front;
    *front = newNode;
    if (*rear == NULL)
        *rear = newNode;
}                                                                                                                        Key Structural Differences                                                                        Data Structure      |      Characteristic      |   Insertion Pointer Behavior                                          
| Doubly Linked List | Bidirectional; supports `prev` and `next`. | Updates both `next` and `prev` pointers.                       
|Circular Linked List| Last node points back to head.  | Updates last node’s `next` to new node and `newNode->next` to head. 
| Queue (LL)       |    FIFO; uses `front` and `rear`.    | Adjusts front pointer when inserting at beginning.                                                                           Code Clarity and Formatting:
-Each function uses meaningful variable names (newNode, head, front, rear).
-Indentation and line spacing make it readable.
-Comments explain each operation clearly.
-Functions are modular and can be independently tested.

ResponseText:
Doubly Linked List -> Create a new node.Set its next to point to the current head.Update the previous pointer of the current head (if it exists) to point to the new node.Make the new node the new head.
struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node* next;
    struct Node* prev;
};

struct Node* insertAtBeginning(struct Node* head, int data) {
    struct Node* newNode = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
    newNode->data = data;
    newNode->next = head;
    newNode->prev = NULL;
    if (head) {
        head->prev = newNode;
    }
    return newNode;
}
Circular Linked List -> Create a new node.If the list is empty, set the new node's next to itself (self-loop).If not, traverse to the last node, update its next to point to the new node, and link the new node's next to the head.Update the head to the new node.
struct Node* insertAtBeginning(struct Node* head, int data) {
    struct Node* newNode = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
    newNode->data = data;
    if (!head) {
        newNode->next = newNode;
        return newNode;
    }
    struct Node* temp = head;
    while (temp->next != head) {
        temp = temp->next;
    }
    temp->next = newNode;
    newNode->next = head;
    return newNode;
}
Queue as LinkedList -> Create a new node.Set its next to the current head.Update the head pointer to the new node.
struct Node* insertAtBeginning(struct Node* head, int data) {
    struct Node* newNode = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
    newNode->data = data;
    newNode->next = head;
    return newNode;
}

